Z||000010||Iron Duke Wellingsley||06-06-2001||09:49 PM||||That open element air cleaner is a "Hot Air" intake.  I don't think you want to do that.<P>The disadvantage here is that fuel atomizes more poorly and air density decreases as temperature increases.  If you introduce air from the engine bay, it could be 200 degrees F or more.  If you get the intake air from the side of the car, it will be half that temperature or less.<P>The stock intake was designed to get cold air on purpose.  You can improve it's flow by eliminating the baffle in the air box, but otherwise it's a good design that needs little modification.<P>I used a 2.25" hole saw to cut through the baffle in the air box.  It changed the sound of the engine a little, but probably did not make much difference in flow.<P>The V6 and 4 cylinder cars use the same airbox, so on a 4 cylinder it should provide plenty of flow.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||06-06-2001||11:55 PM||||I can't understand any advantage to removeing the stock system and putting the open element air filter on the end of a tube just behind the stock intake vent. Wouldn't this actually be worse, since it takes in warm air from the engine bay? I though thats what we were trying to avoid. Could someone please explain to be why so many people are doing this, and claiming it helps?!? Does it help any? It just isn't clicking for me. I would think the best thing to do would just be to remove the water seperator and use a 3" hose/pipe from the vent to bottem of the filter box, and then a 3" hose/pipe with adapter to the throttle body. Would putting a straight pipe from the filter houseing to the v6 throttle body help any, or hurt? I would think if the inside were polished by useing a metal pipe that would increase airflow. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrgone||06-07-2001||04:33 AM||||i agree seanh, some people dont get how intercoolers work, and why cold air adds HP<P>im just wanting to make a sort of straighter path from outside the car to the engine.||||reg||1
